---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import DocPage from '../components/layout/DocPage.astro';
import DocSection from '../components/layout/DocSection.astro';
import { t } from '../i18n/en.ts';
---

<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description="How CapXer handles your camera, your settings and analytics." dockLabel={t.dock.onThisPage}>
  <div slot="dock" class="dock-row">
    <span class="dock-label">{t.dock.onThisPage}</span>
    <a class="dock-tool" href="#camera">Camera</a>
    <a class="dock-tool" href="#storage">Settings</a>
    <a class="dock-tool" href="#analytics">Analytics</a>
  </div>
  <DocPage
    title="Privacy"
    lead="Short version: your camera stays on your device, and analytics only runs if you say yes."
  >
    <DocSection id="camera" title="Camera">
      <p>
        When you use the live camera, the video is processed entirely inside your browser. No video, photo or frame is
        ever uploaded. CapXer has no server that could receive it: the site is a set of static files.
      </p>
      <p>
        The camera only starts when you tap to turn it on, and it is released when you switch back to the scene or
        leave the page. Photos you take are made in your browser and handed straight to you as a file. The level uses
        your device's motion sensors in the same way: the readings never leave the browser. The exposure aids (histogram,
        zebras, false colour and focus peaking) are calculated in your browser from the same video, and nothing about
        them is sent anywhere.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="storage" title="Settings and saved setups">
      <p>
        CapXer remembers your work in your browser's local storage: your last viewfinder setup, the setups you save,
        your own cameras and lenses, the phone or webcam you picked for the live camera, and your exposure-aid choices,
        plus your theme and your analytics choice. All of it stays on this device and is never sent anywhere. Delete it
        with Clear all saved data in the viewfinder's Saved panel, or by clearing this site's data in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>
        Export file saves the same data as a file that you keep. Import file reads such a file inside your browser; it is
        not uploaded.
      </p>
      <p>
        A share link carries one setup (camera, format, frame, focal length and guides) in the part of the address after
        the # sign. Browsers never send that part to a server, so the setup only reaches the people you give the link
        to. If you allow analytics, it only receives the page's address without that part.
      </p>
      <p>
        To work offline and as an installed app, CapXer keeps a copy of its own pages and code in your browser. That copy
        holds no personal data.
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="analytics" title="Analytics">
      <p>
        If you allow it, CapXer uses Google Analytics 4 to count visits and see which pages are used. Google Analytics
        then sets cookies (named <code>_ga</code> and <code>_ga_</code> followed by an ID) and receives information
        such as the pages you view, your browser and device type, and your approximate location. Google signals and
        ad personalisation are turned off.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you decline, Google Analytics is never loaded and no analytics cookies are set. CapXer never sends your
        camera feed, photos or camera and lens settings to analytics.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can change your choice at any time:{' '}
        <button type="button" class="cursor-pointer text-accent underline underline-offset-3" data-open-consent>
          {t.consent.settings}
        </button>
      </p>
    </DocSection>

    <DocSection id="none" title="What CapXer does not have" tone="note">
      <p>No accounts, no ads, no tracking beyond the optional analytics above, and no selling of data.</p>
    </DocSection>
  </DocPage>
</BaseLayout>
